module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_start_limit_outlives_a_unit_file_replacement

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data a_start_limit_outlives_a_unit_file_replacement: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_start_limit_outlives_a_unit_file_replacement" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "A START LIMIT OUTLIVED A UNIT-FILE REPLACEMENT AND REFUSED THE TRANSACTION'S OWN ROLLBACK. systemd keys StartLimitBurst / StartLimitIntervalSec on the unit NAME, not on the unit file's contents, so a transaction that applies a successor unit and then restores the incumbent under the same name has spent the counter on the apply's start. The bounded-experiment unit renders StartLimitBurst=1 over 600 s (gunbc.spark.native_serving_realization arm_unit_directives ArmBoundedExperiment) to mean 'one attempt per launch'; the rollback's `systemctl restart` was a second start inside the window, systemd refused it ('Start request repeated too quickly'), and under `set -e` the rollback stopped with the fence still held: the arm was left down with its disposition unestablished, the outcome rollback exists to prevent.",

    "SPECIMEN, 2026-10-04, Group A nvfp4-8 launch run 37241423753: readback refused the head (the transport-verdict race filed under readiness_read_as_a_single_probe), rollback restored the fp8-16 unit file on all four hosts and every restart failed on the start limit; observe 37242217295 read no containers and zero GPU processes on srv5-srv8. Recovered through the leftover stage's FenceOnly resolution after the 2 h live-transaction horizon (fp8-16 relaunch run 37252898518), by which time the 10-minute window had long expired -- that recovery is not evidence for the repair.",

    "THE REPAIR AND WHY THIS ONE: the transaction clears the counter as a declared step (`systemctl reset-failed <unit>`) IMMEDIATELY before each of its two deliberate starts -- the apply (spark_native_host_apply_script) and the restore (spark_native_rollback_script) -- and nowhere else. The alternative, a limit that does not span a unit-file replacement, would need a fresh unit NAME per incarnation, which breaks the declared-incumbent model the preserve/restore pair relies on. Clearing it at the deliberate start keeps 'one attempt per launch' true: Restart=no and StartLimitBurst=1 are unchanged, so a crash between transactions still meets the limit. Witnesses w_the_restore_clears_the_start_limit_immediately_before_its_own_start, w_the_apply_clears_the_start_limit_immediately_before_its_own_start, and the control w_no_non_starting_transaction_script_clears_the_start_limit (native_serving_resumable_apply_witness_test); the rendered unit's StartLimitBurst=1 is read by native_serving_realization_witness_test. RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable (refused loudly, left the arm down). RUNG NOW: mechanically preventable (script-text witnesses). CEILING: structurally guaranteed -- a start operation carrier whose only constructor pairs the clear with the start. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: a modeled systemd start operation in extdeps.systemd that the transaction consumes instead of spelling `systemctl restart` in shell.",

    "THE FIRST REPAIR WAS ITSELF WRONG ON HARDWARE (2026-10-05, nvfp4-8 retry run 37254736596 from gunbc#13319 bed014277c). The unconditional `systemctl reset-failed <unit>` refused on srv6 with 'Unit gunbc-glm53-stair-head.service not loaded': the launch mode stops the declared incumbent before the transaction, systemd garbage-collects an inactive unit, and reset-failed on an unloaded unit is an error. Under set -e that stopped srv6's apply before its start AND srv6's rollback before its restart, leaving srv6's disposition unread while the three workers restored fp8-16. The script-text witnesses were green because they asserted the clear's PRESENCE, not its behavior against a unit state the transaction itself produces. RE-REPAIR: spark_native_clear_start_limit clears iff list-units reports the unit loaded -- the only state that carries a counter -- and never on is-failed (a cleanly stopped unit is inactive and its counter still refuses) nor with a tolerance; witness w_the_start_limit_clear_is_conditioned_on_a_loaded_unit_and_nothing_else. The residual this exposes: no witness here executes systemctl, so the condition's correctness against systemd's unit lifecycle is still read, not run -- the hardware run is the evidence.",

    "THE CLEAR WAS STILL MISSING AT A THIRD START (side-chat ruling on #13319, finding 4): the enclosing incumbent bracket restored a stopped bounded step with a plain SystemctlStart (gunbc.spark.host_occupancy_admission restore_spark_serving_units), so a launch refused before actuation inside the previous step's window would have been refused restoring it. REPAIR: one operation, gunbc.spark.deliberate_unit_start (read the load state, reset-failed if loaded, restart), as typed HostOperation legs and nothing else -- the incumbent restore calls it, and the transaction's apply and rollback split their scripts around it (spark_native_apply_sequence, spark_native_rollback_sequence; review 76352 removed a shell spelling of the same rows). Witnesses: dag/test/claim/spark/deliberate_unit_start_witness_test.dag -- the restore refuses on a host whose reset-failed fails (a plain start would pass), an unloaded unit is started without a clear, no stage script spells a start, and the apply's route is write, typed start, wait. The script-text witnesses named above are superseded by these. RUNG NOW: mechanically preventable, one realization. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: the start as the only constructor of a started-unit fact the readback consumes.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
